s. Normalized_gain should be a number, presumably float. The example uses numbers like 0.38569502, etc. We'll compute and leave as float.\n\nEdge: if call not parseable, accepted false, but normalized_gain still computed? The description says \"Require both parseability and normalized gain at or above threshold.\" So unparseable calls are not accepted, but we still need to include them in diagnostics with accepted=false and normalized_gain computed anyway? The description says \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order. Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" It doesn't specify skipping unparseable. I think we include all calls in diagnostics, with accepted boolean based on parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold. For normalized_gain, we compute even if unparseable? The formula is defined: subtract loss with call from loss without it, normalize by at least one prediction token. So we compute normalized_gain regardless. Then apply parseable check.\n\nThreshold inclusive: >= threshold.\n\n\"rounded per-call diagnostics\" - does it mean we should round normalized_gain? The output schema says type: number. Usually rounding is not specified. But \"rounded\" might mean we should round to some decimal? The example outputs not provided. Since task mentions \"rounded per-call diagnostics\", maybe we should round to a reasonable number of decimal places? But the schema says \"type: number\" without precision. To be safe, we compute as float and let JSON output naturally. We'll keep as float. The \"rounded\" might just be descriptive.\n\nNo randomness, no extra dependencies.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) that processes calls list.\n\nReturn dict with accepted_call_ids and diagnostics.\n\nMake sure to handle missing fields? According to fixture, all fields present.\n\nWe'll implement with list comprehension.\n\nWrite Python code as a string to be placed in \"implementation\" key.\n\nBe careful with JSON escaping: newlines and backslashes. We'll wr